WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.480
A gram of sweat that evaporates from your skin carries off about 2.4 kJ of heat.

2
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.560
A gram that drips off carries away almost none.

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.460
So the cooling comes from evaporation, not from sweat itself.

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.420
Sweat cools you only as fast as air takes up water vapour.

5
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.420
On a humid day, that can lag behind the heat your muscles make.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:26.543
For a person, heat balance is a sum.

7
00:00:27.263 --> 00:00:32.303
Heat storage equals metabolic rate, minus external work, plus or minus dry exchange,

8
00:00:32.763 --> 00:00:33.963
minus evaporative loss.

9
00:00:34.863 --> 00:00:39.243
Dry exchange means radiant, convective and conductive heat, and it can run either

10
00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:41.383
way. Every term is in watts.

11
00:00:42.123 --> 00:00:44.663
When storage is zero, body temperature is steady.

12
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:51.133
Take illustrative numbers for a healthy 70 kg adult cycling steadily in hot, sunny

13
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:56.733
air. Metabolic rate is 1,000 W, and 20 percent of it becomes external work.

14
00:00:57.313 --> 00:00:59.113
That leaves 800 W of heat.

15
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:03.753
Dry heat gain adds 50 W, and respiratory heat loss is ignored.

16
00:01:04.473 --> 00:01:08.813
To keep storage at zero, evaporation must remove 850 W.

17
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:14.727
Evaporating sweat takes up about 2.43 kJ per g at skin temperature.

18
00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:21.187
So 850 W needs about 1.26 L of sweat per hour, if every gram evaporates.

19
00:01:22.007 --> 00:01:24.927
Suppose only 70 percent evaporates and the rest drips.

20
00:01:25.487 --> 00:01:29.107
The sweat rate then rises to about 1.80 L per hour.

21
00:01:29.107 --> 00:01:33.887
Without drinking, body mass falls about 2.6 percent in that hour.

22
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:42.640
Now let humid air cap evaporation at 700 W, when the body needs 850 The gap of 150 W

23
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.220
is stored as heat, 540 kJ in an hour.

24
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:54.340
Take 2.98 kJ per kg per degree C. Then 70 kg stores about 209 kJ per degree.

25
00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:00.120
If body temperature is uniform, it rises about 2.6 degrees C per hour.

26
00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:05.653
Once evaporation reaches the most that air allows, sweating harder adds dripping,

27
00:02:05.913 --> 00:02:08.573
not cooling. Balance returns three ways.

28
00:02:09.073 --> 00:02:10.613
Make less heat by slowing down.

29
00:02:11.093 --> 00:02:13.573
Take in less dry heat with shade or cooler air.

30
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:14.973
Or have drier air.

31
00:02:15.513 --> 00:02:19.113
These numbers are made up for practice, and the rate predicts no real person.

32
00:02:19.813 --> 00:02:24.473
Keep this rule. Sweat cools only when it evaporates, and the air sets how much can.
